WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.760 position:63%
Why are delivery apps obsessed with AI slop?

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.600 position:63%
You’ve probably seen these around.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.560 position:63%
On DoorDash, you’ll see a restaurant,&nbsp;
whose food is… not quite right.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.080 position:63%
The meat doesn’t look like&nbsp;
meat, the sauce looks… weird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.480 position:63%
and the food is professionally shot,&nbsp;
and looks a little too perfect.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.240 position:63%
It’s AI…

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.080 position:63%
And these AI food photos&nbsp;
are all over delivery apps.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:24.800 position:63%
Everywhere.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:31.520 position:63%
Fake, AI slop, instead of photos of the&nbsp;
actual food. But some of them are… wellll.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.200 position:63%
It turns out, the blame might&nbsp;
actually fall on the delivery apps.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.960 position:63%
Who’ve been caught changing restaurants’&nbsp;
images, without their knowledge.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.160 position:63%
And there’s another important&nbsp;
question… is any of this legal?

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:50.560 position:63%
Is this blatant false advertising?

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.680 position:63%
If you open DoorDash, or any food delivery&nbsp;
app, you'll run into neon-colored drinks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.640 position:63%
rubbery-looking pasta, waxy chicken,&nbsp;
and textures that barely look like food.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.000 position:63%
“It wasn't just unappetising - which, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:14.160 position:63%
it is - but it was also sort of spiritually&nbsp;
unsettling and cosmically unnerving.”

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.760 position:63%
But what’s crazy is how these&nbsp;
apps seem to be pushing them?

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.080 position:63%
Customers on Just Eat, a European&nbsp;
delivery app, found bacon and eggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.440 position:63%
sitting inside a beer glass? Or a loaf of&nbsp;
garlic bread shaped like an actual plane?

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:32.080 position:63%
What the heck? Turns out, Just Eat&nbsp;
was secretly testing a new feature.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.680 position:63%
The company had rolled out an AI to&nbsp;
“help” restaurants display pictures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.320 position:63%
of dishes they hadn't uploaded photos for.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.240 position:63%
But the AI, well… it took names quite literally.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.400 position:63%
The AI mistook a Northern Irish&nbsp;
café's soda bread for… literal soda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.320 position:63%
while a typo turned "plain"&nbsp;
garlic bread into an actual plane.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.320 position:63%
Or a jacket potato became an actual&nbsp;
winter jacket covered in potatoes.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:58.080 position:63%
A tiger prawn dish turned into&nbsp;
a bowl of literal tiger cubs.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.800 position:63%
This is my favorite though. Toad in the Hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.920 position:63%
a classic British dish… Or a&nbsp;
literal toad… in a hole. Yeah.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.920 position:63%
One customer ordered a tomato&nbsp;
and cheese roll but got tinned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.480 position:63%
tomatoes stuffed inside a soft roll&nbsp;
instead. They were refused a refund.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:19.360 position:63%
Another image showed scrambled eggs with the&nbsp;
letter "R" somehow mixed into them. Sorry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.520 position:63%
I meant “Scrabbled egg”.&nbsp;
Okay, that one’s pretty good.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.120 position:63%
How about popcorn chicken? Or…&nbsp;
chicken, popcorn, and fries?

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.840 position:63%
Or... a side order of allergens?

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.040 position:63%
There are way, way more of these, and&nbsp;
it was clearly, a disaster for Just Eat.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.200 position:63%
They apologized, but also claimed&nbsp;
that every single AI-generated image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.240 position:63%
had been checked by a human before going live.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.880 position:63%
You’re saying a human said these were okay?

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:45.920 position:63%
Even this one?

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.560 position:63%
Just Eat had no plans to&nbsp;
abandon this feature though.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.840 position:63%
Just Eat labeled these as AI-generated. But what’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.760 position:63%
crazy is that this seems to be done&nbsp;
without many restaurants' permission?

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.200 position:63%
“Our little takeaway keeps getting new photos&nbsp;
generated on JustEat every time we remove them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.160 position:63%
The only way to stop it is to override it&nbsp;
with your own photos but we don't have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.000 position:63%
time to take presentable photos of 100+ dishes :(”

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.960 position:63%
Crazy. Absolutely crazy. Especially when&nbsp;
these types of photos can deter customers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.160 position:63%
who just think the AI reflects poor food quality.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.640 position:63%
It’s not just… Just Eat either.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.960 position:63%
Back in 2023, a New York pizzeria's "half buffalo&nbsp;
chicken and half BBQ chicken pie" on Uber Eats had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.840 position:63%
been replaced with a picture of what looked&nbsp;
like a literal pie. Like a chicken pot pie.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.560 position:63%
And it wasn’t even the restaurant who added them.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.680 position:63%
Uh, there, they've started using&nbsp;
AI-generated images of your menu. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:45.280 position:63%
for example, a medium pie, the&nbsp;
AI generated, like, a fruit pie.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.160 position:63%
Yeah, we actually already noticed that today.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:48.800 position:63%
Okay.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.240 position:63%
We're in touch with everyone,&nbsp;
trying to get that all...

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:56.320 position:63%
It's insane. It's very... It's truly bizarre.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:02.560 position:63%
Yeah, I honestly really appreciate you calling&nbsp;
because no one has. We happened to see it.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.360 position:63%
I tweeted about it today or yesterday,&nbsp;
and it, like, 30,000 people liked it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:12.160 position:63%
and everybody's saying, everybody's saying&nbsp;
it's a ghost kitchen or the restaurant's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.800 position:63%
choosing this. And I said, no, it's not a ghost&nbsp;
kitchen. It's a real brick-and-mortar store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.680 position:63%
and I cannot imagine that they would choose this.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.880 position:63%
No, we did not.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.640 position:63%
Okay. I just wanted to make sure.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:26.480 position:63%
It’s hard to say if it was Uber&nbsp;
Eats themselves or a third party.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:32.480 position:63%
But in 2023, Uber said "Uber Eats does&nbsp;
not use AI to add images to the app."

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:38.480 position:63%
But then in July 2025, Uber announced AI tools&nbsp;
to improve food photos by fixing their lighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.840 position:63%
quality, framing, and even how the food looks.

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:42.880 position:63%
DoorDash too.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:48.080 position:63%
Its "AI-enhanced" label means food in photos&nbsp;
can be rearranged, backgrounds can be changed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.160 position:63%
missing parts can be filled in, and&nbsp;
even the camera angle can be altered.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:58.160 position:63%
In April 2026, it introduced features called&nbsp;
AI Retouch, AI Replate, and Match the Style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.760 position:63%
claiming that millions of food&nbsp;
photos had already been improved.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.720 position:63%
At least some of these&nbsp;
don’t alter the food itself.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:10.240 position:63%
For obvious reasons, there are a ton of&nbsp;
problems with all these AI slop food photos.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:15.120 position:63%
For example, a 2025 study found that when&nbsp;
people knew a food image was AI-generated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.120 position:63%
they valued the food less and were more&nbsp;
likely to speak negatively about it.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:23.360 position:63%
"AI-generated imagery may diminish&nbsp;
pleasure and heighten perceived risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:26.480 position:63%
leading to less favourable&nbsp;
consumer responses. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:31.440 position:63%
food marketers should ensure that AI-generated&nbsp;
images retain a realistic and appetising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.240 position:63%
appearance to prevent negative effects on&nbsp;
perceived value and brand evaluations."

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.720 position:63%
Psychologist Alexander Diel’s team found&nbsp;
that people thought AI-generated food looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:46.080 position:63%
just as healthy as real food, but were&nbsp;
less interested in actually eating it.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:52.560 position:63%
But, there is something to consider. You and I&nbsp;
likely can spot most of these, but a 2023 study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:58.880 position:63%
found that more than 60% of people couldn't tell&nbsp;
whether a food photo was real or AI-generated.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.520 position:63%
AI has only gotten better since then.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:07.840 position:63%
Even still, if some people can’t see it, that&nbsp;
doesn’t mean it’s good to use. More on that later.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.280 position:63%
But even outside of these&nbsp;
choices by the apps themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:14.480 position:63%
some restaurants do use AI for their photos.

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:15.920 position:63%
So why?

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.000 position:63%
Well, these delivery apps have created a system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.600 position:63%
that rewards mass amounts of&nbsp;
photos, even if they’re fake.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:28.720 position:63%
And, this has been exploited by another&nbsp;
hidden part of the takeout industry.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:40.320 position:63%
The obvious answer is cost and time, but the&nbsp;
picture becomes clearer when we look at how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.640 position:63%
these delivery apps push restaurants&nbsp;
toward volume. In more ways than one.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:49.680 position:63%
DoorDash's numbers show that 38% of customers&nbsp;
are influenced by menu photos when choosing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.640 position:63%
where to order. Restaurants with photos&nbsp;
can also see up to 44% more monthly sales.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:59.280 position:63%
If at least 50% of the menu has&nbsp;
photos, sales increase by 13%.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:03.680 position:63%
DoorDash also grades restaurants, with a&nbsp;
“merchant status”, ranging from “most loved”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:09.360 position:63%
to “poor”. Restaurants that are “most loved” get&nbsp;
a badge and a dedicated spot on the homepage.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.360 position:63%
The grade is based on a few things,&nbsp;
but the biggest is “Photo Coverage”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:16.320 position:63%
Meaning the more photos, the&nbsp;
better your restaurant ranks.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:19.600 position:63%
DoorDash offers restaurants free photoshoots… but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:25.200 position:63%
you can’t use those photos on any other&nbsp;
third-party platform. Aka, other apps.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:30.800 position:63%
Uber Eats charges around $125 for a&nbsp;
quick shoot. Very cheap for a photoshoot.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:32.800 position:63%
This is actually a problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.480 position:63%
as it can lead to disappointing&nbsp;
results and less professional shoots.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:42.080 position:63%
"If there's anyone to blame for this race to&nbsp;
the bottom of food photography, it's the apps."

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:47.040 position:63%
So as a restaurant, you try to take photos of your&nbsp;
food, which look washed out and unprofessional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:52.160 position:63%
because you’re not a photographer. The app&nbsp;
offers a photoshoot, which disappoints,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:55.360 position:63%
again, through no fault of the photographers.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:00.320 position:63%
Some then see AI as a way to “touch up”&nbsp;
the photos, or just replace them entirely.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:02.720 position:63%
But another part of the delivery ecosystem has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:06.560 position:63%
welcomed these AI tools with&nbsp;
open arms: Ghost Kitchens.

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:10.880 position:63%
You’ve probably heard of these, and they&nbsp;
were a huge story around the pandemic.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:13.440 position:63%
Some also call them “Cloud Kitchens”.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:17.920 position:63%
But if you haven’t, imagine one kitchen…&nbsp;
which is simultaneously acting as 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:22.880 position:63%
or in the past as many as 20, or even 30&nbsp;
different restaurants on delivery apps.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:29.040 position:63%
Eats365, a company that sells systems to these&nbsp;
kitchens, says “a small space can translate into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:33.760 position:63%
large profits! With a compact solution that&nbsp;
lets you maximize your chefs’ efficiency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:38.000 position:63%
improve your operational productivity&nbsp;
and expand your brand’s online presence”.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.080 position:63%
What’s really interesting, though,&nbsp;
is they’ve been impacted by AI.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:46.400 position:63%
DoorDash allows restaurants to run up to 10&nbsp;
different virtual brands from one kitchen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:52.080 position:63%
But each brand needs its own header image,&nbsp;
and every menu item needs a distinct photo.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:57.040 position:63%
So, imagine one person running a single&nbsp;
kitchen with ten different restaurant listings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:02.080 position:63%
possibly selling the same burger, but&nbsp;
each listing needs its own picture.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:07.760 position:63%
Back in February 2024, 404 Media found dozens&nbsp;
of ghost kitchens on DoorDash and Grubhub&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:13.680 position:63%
using AI-generated food images. Some showed&nbsp;
food that couldn't even exist in real life.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.920 position:63%
The reporter actually ordered one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:18.560 position:63%
worst-looking cheesesteaks&nbsp;
to see what would arrive.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:22.400 position:63%
And what arrived was totally&nbsp;
different from the shiny AI pictures.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:27.520 position:63%
Honestly, the real thing actually looks&nbsp;
pretty good! It looks like real food.

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:31.280 position:63%
But there's a much bigger business&nbsp;
behind these virtual restaurants.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:36.160 position:63%
An NBC News investigation found that more than&nbsp;
one in five Uber Eats listings in a sample of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:40.480 position:63%
1,656 New York City restaurants&nbsp;
appeared to be virtual brands.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:44.320 position:63%
The same trend showed up across&nbsp;
all 12 cities NBC examined.

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:47.680 position:63%
One New York restaurant owner was&nbsp;
running more than 60 virtual brands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:52.320 position:63%
across delivery platforms, all out of&nbsp;
his physical restaurant, Spinners Pizza.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.720 position:63%
According to him, those extra listings&nbsp;
were what kept his business afloat.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:01.360 position:63%
“The rest of the week isn’t enough to support&nbsp;
the rent, the workers, the electricity.”

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:04.560 position:63%
In another case, 12 different&nbsp;
restaurant listings were selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:08.000 position:63%
identical breakfast burritos&nbsp;
from the same sports bar kitchen.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:12.000 position:63%
So, for restaurants managing multiple&nbsp;
outlets and trying to cut expenses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.560 position:63%
AI becomes an easy shortcut.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:17.600 position:63%
But in China, things have gone even further.

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:20.800 position:63%
Some merchants were using AI&nbsp;
to fake storefront photos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:26.240 position:63%
showing packed dining rooms and long queues, even&nbsp;
when the actual business was just a small kitchen.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.440 position:63%
It got so bad that Meituan, a major delivery app,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:32.960 position:63%
set up a task force to tackle&nbsp;
the problem and warn users.

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:38.560 position:63%
But there's already a market for it. People are&nbsp;
selling custom AI-generated storefront images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:43.440 position:63%
sometimes called "AI storefront&nbsp;
avatars," for around 40 yuan. Crazy!

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:48.240 position:63%
So, there’s one big obvious question&nbsp;
here: Is this false advertising?

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:53.760 position:63%
If you buy a meal with an AI image, which looks&nbsp;
nothing like the actual product… Is that legal?

00:10:53.760 --> 00:11:04.240 position:63%
And if it isn’t… What do we do about it?

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:09.680 position:63%
If you’ve watched this far, you probably&nbsp;
like our videos. So consider subscribing!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:13.200 position:63%
It’s free and means you see&nbsp;
our videos when they come out.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:14.960 position:63%
This seems cut and dry.

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:20.400 position:63%
To be honest, if THIS is what the food really&nbsp;
looks like, I sure hope it’s false advertising.

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:22.240 position:63%
But where is the line?

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:24.080 position:63%
And can we enforce it?

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:27.600 position:63%
The problem is that the FTC&nbsp;
doesn’t really police this.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:31.200 position:63%
The FTC, the agency responsible&nbsp;
for protecting consumers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:36.080 position:63%
has no specific rules for food photos.&nbsp;
But it does for general advertising.

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:40.400 position:63%
“the net impression of any advertisement, which&nbsp;
includes photographs, other graphic elements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:43.840 position:63%
and text, must be truthful and non-misleading.”

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:49.920 position:63%
So why hasn’t the FTC gone after food&nbsp;
photos? Well… food is inexpensive.

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:55.040 position:63%
“It isn’t surprising. The commission is unlikely&nbsp;
to take law-enforcement actions in cases where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:00.000 position:63%
consumers can easily evaluate the product, it's&nbsp;
inexpensive, and it's frequently purchased.”

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.880 position:63%
So, most of the time, customers have to&nbsp;
take these companies to court themselves.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:06.960 position:63%
That's what happened with Burger King.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:10.960 position:63%
Where customers got a smaller burger&nbsp;
than what was advertised in Whopper ads.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:13.680 position:63%
A judge allowed a lawsuit&nbsp;
over this to move forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:17.760 position:63%
But a similar case against McDonald's&nbsp;
and Wendy's was thrown out in 2023.

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:21.120 position:63%
In the UK, the rules are a&nbsp;
little clearer. Companies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:24.880 position:63%
still responsible for misleading&nbsp;
ads, even when AI creates them.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:29.040 position:63%
The advertising regulator has already&nbsp;
banned an AI-generated advert for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:32.080 position:63%
robot puppy that showed it doing&nbsp;
things the real product couldn't.

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:36.160 position:63%
But it hasn't made a similar&nbsp;
ruling over AI food photos.

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:39.520 position:63%
In New Zealand, someone complained&nbsp;
about a burger advert where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:44.000 position:63%
burgers looked nothing like the real&nbsp;
ones and even seemed to defy gravity.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:47.120 position:63%
But the regulator decided not to take action.

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:51.920 position:63%
And in the EU, new rules introduced in&nbsp;
August 2026 require certain realistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:56.240 position:63%
AI-generated images to be labeled as&nbsp;
AI. Companies that break the rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.240 --> 00:13:01.440 position:63%
could face fines of up to €15 million&nbsp;
or 3% of their global annual turnover.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:06.720 position:63%
So what do we do? Well, I think we should look&nbsp;
at an example that’s been around for years.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:11.040 position:63%
Traditional food photography has used fake&nbsp;
ingredients to make food look more enticing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.520 position:63%
appealing, or like a better value product.

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:18.080 position:63%
That perfect coffee foam? Usually&nbsp;
it’s dish soap mixed with coffee.

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:23.840 position:63%
And the coffee itself is often fake too. Some&nbsp;
food stylists use soy sauce with the soap foam.

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:28.320 position:63%
That glass of white wine? It could just be&nbsp;
water with a few drops of brown coloring.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:31.920 position:63%
Burgers are sometimes held together with&nbsp;
toothpicks and cardboard to make them look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:37.040 position:63%
bigger. And the meat might not even be fully&nbsp;
cooked, because fully cooked patties shrink.

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:41.920 position:63%
Even the ice cubes in drink adverts are often&nbsp;
fake. They're made of plastic or acrylic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:44.480 position:63%
so they don't melt under hot studio lights.

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:49.120 position:63%
And when it comes to meat, stylists sometimes&nbsp;
use blowtorches to brown the outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:51.760 position:63%
then add coloring to make it look freshly grilled.

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:56.560 position:63%
Food photography, minus AI, is actually&nbsp;
really fascinating, and its own art form.

00:13:56.560 --> 00:14:00.240 position:63%
But what’s the difference?&nbsp;
Well, there is a big one.

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.640 position:63%
Traditional food photography might involve a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:07.360 position:63%
of tricks to make food look better.&nbsp;
But the food itself is still real.

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:12.000 position:63%
You can fake the presentation, but the&nbsp;
actual product you're selling should be real.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:14.960 position:63%
If you're advertising milk, you use real milk.

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:18.320 position:63%
If you're advertising ice&nbsp;
cream, you use real ice cream.

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:22.400 position:63%
But if the ice cream is just sitting in the&nbsp;
background of another advert, stylists can use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:27.200 position:63%
mashed potatoes or frosting instead, because&nbsp;
they won't melt under the studio lights.

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:30.160 position:63%
And this has been a gradually evolving practice.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:34.480 position:63%
Back in 1968, Campbell's was putting&nbsp;
glass marbles at the bottom of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:39.120 position:63%
soup bowls to push the vegetables closer to&nbsp;
the surface, making the soup look fuller.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:42.400 position:63%
So their competitor, Heinz, complained to the FTC.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:46.560 position:63%
An investigation followed, and Campbell's&nbsp;
eventually agreed to stop using the trick.

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:50.240 position:63%
So, traditional food photography&nbsp;
might dress up the product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:53.600 position:63%
but… most of the time it is the product.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:55.280 position:63%
AI food… isn’t.

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:58.720 position:63%
For AI… we’re still in the&nbsp;
middle of this early stage.

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:02.560 position:63%
We’re still figuring out where to draw the&nbsp;
line on what’s an acceptable “touch up”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:04.720 position:63%
and what’s false advertising.

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:07.440 position:63%
But, we should probably draw that line soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:11.520 position:63%
because I’m a bit sick of seeing&nbsp;
all these photos on DoorDash.

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:14.480 position:63%
Have you also noticed it’s not&nbsp;
just delivery apps, though?

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:18.160 position:63%
A ton of restaurants or all kinds&nbsp;
of small businesses will use AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:24.160 position:63%
for anything and everything. Posters,&nbsp;
social media, menus, sidewalk signs.

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:28.560 position:63%
In fact, small businesses in particular&nbsp;
seem to be obsessed with AI slop.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:31.120 position:63%
To learn why, watch this video next.

